(* Beginning of Notebook Content *)
Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " \n",
 "Advanced Lesson 15\n\nContexts\n\nDr. Dale R. Doty\nUniversity of Tulsa"
}], "Title"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Packages vastly expand the functionality of ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " by adding new functions.  \nHowever, confusion is often introduced by the \
context associated with each package."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.389942226726*^9, 3.38994224756*^9}}],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["  Contexts:", "Subtitle"]], "Section"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{" ", 
  RowBox[{"Remove", "[", "\"\<Global`*\>\"", " ", "]"}], " "}]], "InputOnly"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " has carefully chosen a different name for each of its internal functions.  \
However, when new functionality is added by opening up a package (either \
standard to ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " or written by another person), there is the possibility of the same name \
from different packages colliding with each other.  One way around this is to \
attach to each name the package with which it is associated.  Then it is \
impossible for two different commands with the same name (but from different \
packages) to have the same total name.  However, it is the responsibility of \
the user to indicate which package the name refers to.  \n"
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["  What Is A Context?", "Subsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A context in ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " is like a subdirectory on a computer hard disk.  Each subdirectory has its \
own context (name).  Files in different subdirectories with the same name are \
never confused as long as the file name and subdirectory are both specified.  \
\nJust like MS Windows: when you boot your computer, the directory   \n\t\
C:\\Users\\Name\\Documents\\ ...   \nis the automatically the default \
directory for your user created work.  The corresponding analogy in ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " that contains your user created work is context:  \n\tGlobal` ... \nAlso, \
just like MS Windows, the Windows directory   \n\tC:\\Windows   \nis the \
operating system directory.  The corresponding analogy in ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " that contains all system functions is context:  \n\tSystem` ...\nIf you \
have two different files with the same name, \"name.exe\", but in different \
directories, they are never confused when you specify their FULL name, For \
example:\n\n\tC:\\Users\\Name\\Documents\\name.exe\n\t\nis never confused \
with: \n\t\n\tC:\\Windows\\name.exe\n\t"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.389942372354*^9, 3.38994240421*^9}, {3.389942437624*^9, 
  3.3899424700030003`*^9}, {3.3899425283050003`*^9, 3.389942635528*^9}, {
  3.3899427316070004`*^9, 3.389942746926*^9}, {3.389942777465*^9, 
  3.389943279611*^9}, {3.389953631734*^9, 3.389953780231*^9}, {
  3.389953816415*^9, 3.389953951101*^9}, {3.3899539848380003`*^9, 
  3.3899540071210003`*^9}, {3.389954215157*^9, 3.389954221436*^9}}],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The same is true for a symbol in ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica.",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " If you have two different symbols with the same name, \"x\", but in \
different contexts, they are never confused when you specify their FULL name, \
For example:\n\n\tGlobal` x\n\t\nis never confused with: \n\t\n\tSystem` x\n\t\
\nWhen you search for a file that has an \"incomplete\" name like \"My \
Report.doc\", MS Windows searches a Directory Path which determines the order \
in which subdirectories will be searched when looking for the file \"My \
Report.doc\". Windows will take the FIRST occurrence that it finds, even if \
it is NOT the one that you wanted. Likewise, ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " will search for an \"incomplete\" name like \"x\", by searching a Context \
Path which determines the order in which contexts will be searched when \
looking for the symbol \"x\".  ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " will take the FIRST occurrence that it finds, even if it is NOT the one \
that you wanted. Please keep this in mind when you seem to get the incorrect \
value for a symbol in ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ".\n\nFinally, just like MS Windows which has a current default \
subdirectory, at any moment in time ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " has a current default context."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.389942372354*^9, 3.38994240421*^9}, {3.389942437624*^9, 
  3.3899424700030003`*^9}, {3.3899425283050003`*^9, 3.389942635528*^9}, {
  3.3899427316070004`*^9, 3.389942746926*^9}, {3.389942777465*^9, 
  3.389943279611*^9}, {3.389953631734*^9, 3.389953780231*^9}, {
  3.389953816415*^9, 3.389953951101*^9}, {3.3899539848380003`*^9, 
  3.389954141973*^9}, {3.389954189064*^9, 3.389954193032*^9}, {
  3.389954230593*^9, 3.3899542755439997`*^9}, {3.389954334238*^9, 
  3.38995464315*^9}, {3.389954692844*^9, 3.389954893027*^9}}]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["  What Is The Notation For A Context?", "Subsection"],

Cell["\<\
Context names are character strings terminated with a back quote.  
  
\tFor example:     newContext`
  
The back quote is located in the upper left hand corner of the keyboard.\
\>", "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["  What Is The Current Context?", "Subsection"],

Cell[BoxData["$Context"], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\n\"Global`\" is the default context that opens up when you start ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ".  Unless you specifically switch to a new context, all definitions and \
assignments that you make will be stored in context \"Global`\".  For \
example, define a new variable, assign a value to it and then determine what \
information ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " has on this symbol name:"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5233621684395013`*^9}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"newVariable", "=", "222"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"??", "newVariable"}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Note that we created a new variable and assigned a value.  Observe that the \
new variable's name has been associated with the context Global`.  The full \
name of the new variable is now:  
\t\"Global`newVariable\"\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.389955012407*^9, 3.389955020442*^9}, 
   3.5233622016698337`*^9}]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["  What Is The Context Path?", "Subsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The context path determines the search order when ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " attempts to locate a symbol or command when its full name is NOT given.  \
Examine the search path order shown below which is organized from left to \
right.  In general both context \"Global`\" and context \"System` will be in \
the search path so using the short name will always allow it to be located.  \
Also, this explains why it is extremely important to NEVER define a symbol \
(variable, function, etc.) with the same name as a system command.  This can \
ALWAYS be accomplished by starting all of your symbols with a lower case \
character.  ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica ",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "ALWAYS starts its symbols with an upper case character or a $. "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.5236182622138352`*^9, 3.5236184238254514`*^9}, {
  3.5236184802960157`*^9, 3.523618602067234*^9}, {3.523618664747861*^9, 
  3.5236186771779847`*^9}}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"  ", "$ContextPath"}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

It is possible to determine the context associated with a variable or command:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5233622400002165`*^9}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Context", " ", "[", "newVariable", "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Context", " ", "[", "Plot", "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Context", " ", "[", "Integrate", "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Context", " ", "[", "\[Pi]", "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\nMost objects built into ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " are stored in context:  \"System`\".  Unless otherwise specified, user \
defined objects are stored in context:   Global`.\n\n Note that the current \
context is  Global`."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5233623518413353`*^9}],

Cell[BoxData["$Context"], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["  Where Does Mathematica Store A Newly Created Symbol?", "Subsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "When you ask ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " to find a symbol, it checks if the symbol has been created in the current \
context (usually  Global`).  If the symbol can not be located in the current \
context, then each context specified by the \"$ContextPath\" will be searched \
in the order specified.  If the symbol still can not be found, then \
Mathematica automatically creates that symbol in the current context (unless \
another context assignment is explicitly made)."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"  ", 
  RowBox[{"newSymbol", "=", "\"\<Default assignment\>\""}]}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{" ", 
  RowBox[{"Context", " ", "[", "newSymbol", "]"}]}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"??", "newSymbol"}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Next, specify a new context and create the same symbol with a completely \
different value.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.523362421702034*^9}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"newContext`newSymbol", "=", " ", 
  "\"\<Completely different value\>\""}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Check the current default context:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5233624372721896`*^9}],

Cell[BoxData["$Context"], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Ask what context \"newSymbol\" is in:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.523362438632203*^9}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{" ", 
  RowBox[{"Context", " ", "[", "newSymbol", "]"}]}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Ask for information on \"newSymbol\" :\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5233624402922196`*^9}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"??", "newSymbol"}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Observe that \"newSymbol\" was:
\tfound in context:  Global` 
\thaving a full name of:  Global`newSymbol   
\tand assigned the value: \"Default assignment\"

Search for the new symbol \"newSymbol\" in all available contexts.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.389955225575*^9, 3.389955234282*^9}}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"??", "*`newSymbol"}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Observe that it was located in context:  newContext`  as well as the default \
context (currently  Global`).

Finally, we can determine the assignment to the new symbol in the new context \
by specifying both the context and the symbol.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5233624998428154`*^9}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"??", "newContext`newSymbol"}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

However, it has a different assignment in context  Global`:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.3899552689049997`*^9}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"??", "  ", "Global`newSymbol"}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

If only the symbol's name is specified, then only its value in the default \
context (currently  Global`) will be used:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.389955278507*^9}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"??", "  ", "newSymbol"}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

As you can see, if our new symbol is modified or deleted, only the assignment \
in the default context will be effected.  The other assignments in other \
contexts will remain unchanged. \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.389955296098*^9}]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["  How Do You Switch To A New Default Context?", "Subsection"],

Cell["\<\
Before switching the default context, I will create four new variables (in \
two different contexts):\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Global`symbolGlobal", "=", " ", "\"\<Only in Global\>\""}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{" ", 
  RowBox[{
  "Global`symbolBoth", " ", "=", " ", 
   "\"\<Its value in  Global`\>\""}]}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"newContext`symbolBoth", "=", " ", 
  "\"\<Its value in newContext`\>\""}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"newContext`symbolNewContext", "=", " ", 
  "\"\<Only in newContext`\>\""}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Next, without specifying the context, determine the assigned values.  The \
default context is:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.523362573603553*^9}],

Cell[BoxData["$Context"], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"??", "symbolGlobal"}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Which is located as expected.

Note that newContext` is NOT in the search path:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.523618964870862*^9, 3.52361897369095*^9}, {
  3.523619427905492*^9, 3.5236194400156136`*^9}, {3.523619506756281*^9, 
  3.523619529426507*^9}}],

Cell[BoxData["$ContextPath"], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Find information on:  symbolBoth   (which has two different assigned values):\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.389955338291*^9}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"??", "symbolBoth"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"  ", 
  RowBox[{"Context", " ", "[", "symbolBoth", "]"}]}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Note that  \"symbolBoth\" has the value assigned in the default context \
(currently context  Global`) and its value in newContext` was not found.

Next, attempt to find information on the symbol created in the new context:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.5233625770335875`*^9, {3.5236195701769147`*^9, 3.523619594227155*^9}}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"??", "symbolNewContext"}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Note that:   \"symbolNewContext\"   was not found.  Why?\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.389955363455*^9}],

Cell[BoxData["$ContextPath"], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Note that:    \"newContext`\"  is not in the search path (unless you have \
executed this notebook more than once in this session).  However, it is still \
defined by using its full name.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.5233625795536127`*^9, {3.5236196300275135`*^9, 3.523619637487588*^9}}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"??", "newContext`symbolNewContext"}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

It is possible to switch to a new default context by using the \"Begin\" \
command.  This command switches to the new context, but does not change the \
search path.  It also remembers the previously assigned default context and \
switches back to it with the \"End\" command.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.389955385057*^9}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{" ", 
  RowBox[{"Begin", " ", "[", "\"\<newContext`\>\"", "]"}]}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Check the current default context:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5233625818736353`*^9}],

Cell[BoxData["$Context"], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Check the current context path.  
Note that Global` is in the path but newContext` is not:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.523362583383651*^9, {3.523619825459468*^9, 3.523619875629969*^9}}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"  ", "$ContextPath"}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Check the assignment to:  symbolGlobal:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5233625851536684`*^9, 3.52361988770009*^9}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"??", "symbolGlobal"}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Note that \"symbolGlobal\" was found in context Global` because of the path \
assignment.

Now check the assignment to:  symbolBoth

Will it be found in context Global` or newContext`?\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.5233625868536854`*^9, {3.523619904800261*^9, 3.5236199936511497`*^9}}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"??", "symbolBoth"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"  ", 
  RowBox[{"Context", " ", "[", "symbolBoth", "]"}]}]], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\nNote that \"symbolBoth\" was found in context \"Global`\" because of the \
search path. Even though \"symbolBoth\"  also appears in context \
\"newContext`\" which is the current default context.  ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " will use the first assignment it locates (path first, default second).\n\n\
To see this, remove Global`symbolBoth but not ",
 "newContext`symbolBoth:"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.5233625885837026`*^9, {3.523620042611639*^9, 3.5236200452516656`*^9}, {
   3.5236200826620398`*^9, 3.52362008568207*^9}, {3.5236201343225565`*^9, 
   3.523620165602869*^9}, {3.5236201976531897`*^9, 3.523620263073844*^9}, {
   3.523620351154724*^9, 3.5236203759549723`*^9}, {3.5236204125053377`*^9, 
   3.523620424165455*^9}}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Remove", "[", "Global`symbolBoth", "]"}]], "InputOnly",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5236211475126877`*^9}],

Cell["\<\

and ask again:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.5236203108043213`*^9, 3.523620323314446*^9}}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"??", "symbolBoth"}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.5236204036552496`*^9, 3.523620403895252*^9}}],

Cell["\<\

it was NOT in the path, but was located in the default context.

Finally, locate symbolNewContext:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.523620450485718*^9, 3.5236205296565094`*^9}}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"??", "symbolNewContext"}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Note that \"symbolNewContext\" was found in context \"newContext`\" because \
\"newContext`\" is the default context  (even though context \"newContext`\" \
is not in the path).\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.3899554380959997`*^9}],

Cell["\<\

I can now switch back automatically:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.389955443391*^9, 3.3899554438120003`*^9}}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{" ", 
  RowBox[{"End", " ", "[", "]"}]}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Check the current default context:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5233625919537363`*^9}],

Cell[BoxData["$Context"], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Check the current context path:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5233625935537524`*^9}],

Cell[BoxData["$ContextPath"], "Input"],

Cell["\<\


Sometimes, when using very large packages, it is desirable to switch to the \
new context and explicitly exclude context  \"Global`\".  The command that \
accomplishes this task is BeginPackage.  This command switches the default \
context, and in addition modifies the path to contain only the contexts \
specified by the arguments.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.389955459369*^9}],

Cell["Check the current default context:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData["$Context"], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Check the current context path:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.523362596663783*^9}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"  ", "$ContextPath"}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Use the BeginPackage command with the new context:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5233625983438005`*^9}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"BeginPackage", "[", "\"\<newContext`\>\"", "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Check the current default context:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.523362603073848*^9}],

Cell[BoxData["$Context"], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Check the current context path:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.523362604823865*^9}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"  ", "$ContextPath"}]], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\nNote that context \"Global`\" is no longer in the path.  \nNote that \
context System` had to remain in the path for ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " to run."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5233626067538843`*^9}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"??", "symbolGlobal"}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Note that \"symbolGlobal\" can no longer be found because context \"Global`\" \
is no longer in the path.  

However, the current value of:  symbolNewContext\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.389955499882*^9, 3.5236207092783055`*^9}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"??", "symbolNewContext"}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5236207133883467`*^9}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"  ", 
  RowBox[{"Context", " ", "[", "symbolBoth", "]"}]}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Note that  \"symbolBoth\" has the value assigned in the default context:  \
newContext`.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.523362611443931*^9, {3.5236207461686745`*^9, 3.523620757388787*^9}}],

Cell["\<\

I can now switch back automatically:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.389955534906*^9, 3.38995553535*^9}}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"  ", 
  RowBox[{"EndPackage", "[", "]"}]}]], "InputOnly"],

Cell["Check the current default context:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData["$Context"], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Check the current context path:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5233626163239803`*^9}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"  ", "$ContextPath"}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Note that the new default context is \"Global`\".  However, after the switch \
back, the path contains the previous context \"newContext`\" along with all \
of the contexts contained in the previous path.  Thus, a symbol like \
\"symbolBoth\" might have two locations.  Note that its value used will be \
determined by its location in the search path.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.523362617413991*^9, {3.523620843319646*^9, 3.52362085669978*^9}, {
   3.523620912170335*^9, 3.523620915790371*^9}, {3.523620961240825*^9, 
   3.523620989511108*^9}}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"??", "symbolBoth"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"  ", 
  RowBox[{"Context", " ", "[", "symbolBoth", "]"}]}]], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["  How Do I Clear And Remove All User Defined Symbols?", "Subsection"],

Cell["\<\
If all of your work has been in the default context \"Global`\", then all of \
your user defined symbols will be assigned to this context.  Thus, you can \
display all of your user defined variables by:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"?", "Global`*"}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

If you want to clear assignments to the symbols in context Global` (without \
removing either the names or the attributes), then you can use:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.523621189533108*^9}],

Cell["Clear[\"Global`*\" ]", "Program"],

Cell["\<\
If you want to clear assignments and any attributes for the symbols in \
context Global` (without removing the names), then you can use:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["ClearAll[\"Global`*\" ]", "Program"],

Cell["Try this out:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"a", "=", "3"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"f", "[", "x_", "]"}], ":=", 
  "\"\<Assignment for f\>\""}]], "InputOnly"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"  ", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"Attributes", " ", "[", "f", "]"}], "=", 
   RowBox[{"{", " ", "HoldAll", "}"}]}]}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"??", "a"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"??", "f"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"ClearAll", " ", "[", "\"\<Global`*\>\"", " ", "]"}]], "InputOnly"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"??", "a"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"??", "f"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"?", "Global`*"}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Note that this procedure cleared all assignments and attributes.  However, it \
did not remove the name for each symbol.  Thus, if you want a symbol in a \
package (and you do not call it with its context) then the residual \
unassigned name in context \"Global`\" could be located and used in \
preference to the package symbol.   What is needed is a command that will \
remove everything.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.5236212413736267`*^9, 3.5236212688639016`*^9}}],

Cell["\<\
If you want to clear assignments, any attributes and the name for symbols in \
context Global` , then you can use:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"  ", 
  RowBox[{"Remove", "[", "\"\<Global`*\>\"", " ", "]"}]}]], "InputOnly"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"?", "Global`*"}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Try this out.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5236213168143806`*^9}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"a", "=", "3"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"f", "[", "x_", "]"}], ":=", "\"\<Assignment for f\>\""}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"  ", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"Attributes", " ", "[", "f", "]"}], "=", 
   RowBox[{"{", " ", "HoldAll", "}"}]}]}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"??", "a"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"??", "f"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"  ", 
  RowBox[{"Remove", "[", "\"\<Global`*\>\"", " ", "]"}]}]], "InputOnly"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"??", "a"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"??", "f"}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

However, all symbols defined in other contexts remain unchanged.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.52362135369475*^9}],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"??", "newContext`symbolNewContext"}]], "Input"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox[" Exercises:", "Title"],
 "  Advanced Lesson 15 "
}], "Section"],

Cell["\<\
Your Name:

Your Math Class:\
\>", "Program",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.390368232619*^9, 3.3903682391219997`*^9}, {
   3.390368866851*^9, 3.390368908736*^9}, {3.3903690435030003`*^9, 
   3.390369082222*^9}, 3.3903880065649595`*^9},
 FontSize->16,
 FontWeight->"Bold"],

Cell["1:  What Is The Current Context?", "Text"],

Cell["2:  What Is The Current Context Path?", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
3:  Create a new symbol (with name: \"newSymbol\") in the current context and \
assign it the value:  \"Current\".\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
4:  Specify a new context (with name: newContext) and create the same symbol \
with a completely different value of: \"New\".\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["5:  Ask for information on \"newSymbol\"  in both contexts", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
6:  Why must you use the Remove command (rather than just the Clear or \
ClearAll commands) on a symbol listed in context Global` if you want to \
access that same symbol in a different context (assume that the default \
context is Global`) ?\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Print",
  FontSize->14,
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 StyleBox[" your homework exercises",
  FontSize->14],
 ".\n",
 StyleBox["But do ",
  FontSize->14,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
 StyleBox["NOT",
  FontSize->18,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
 StyleBox[" print the above lesson along with the exercises!",
  FontSize->14,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]]
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.3903491193324003`*^9, 3.3903491706104*^9}, {
